course: everyday
lesson: 221
title: Respect for everyone, greetings, goodbyes and the table
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every worker deserves the same respect, so you greet the school caretaker, cleaner or dinner lady as kindly as the head teacher, and say please and thank you to staff in shops and cafés.
explain: Greetings differ: a handshake is firm but not crushing, a quick "Good, thanks!" answers "How's it going?" in Canada and the USA, and in New Zealand the hongi, pressing noses and foreheads, is a traditional Maori greeting of shared breath, done when you are invited.
explain: Goodbyes differ too: "See ya", "Cheers", "Take care" and "Catch you later"; and when a visitor leaves your home, you walk them to the door.
explain: At the table you wait until everyone is seated and served, eat quietly, wait for food to be offered, and ask before taking the last piece.
explain: A boy in Leeds is given chopsticks at a friend's dinner; he tries them, never stands them upright in his rice, and politely asks for a fork when the noodles keep slipping.
fact: You greet every worker, like a caretaker or cleaner, as kindly as the head teacher.
fact: A good handshake is firm but not hard, for about two or three shakes; a limp, floppy one can seem unfriendly or not interested.
fact: The hongi is a traditional Maori greeting in New Zealand, done when you are invited.
fact: "How's it going?" said while walking past is a greeting, and a quick "Good, thanks!" is enough.
fact: You introduce people clearly, for example "This is my stepdad, Mark".
fact: Goodbyes vary, like "See ya", "Cheers", "Take care" or "Catch you later"; in Britain and Australia "Cheers" can also mean thanks.
fact: When a visitor leaves your home, you walk them to the door and say goodbye.
fact: You say "please" and "thank you" to staff in shops and cafés.
fact: To ask a stranger something, you start with "Excuse me".
fact: You wait until everyone is seated and served before eating, unless the host says to start.
fact: You ask if anyone else would like the last piece before taking it.
fact: You eat quietly and wait until food is offered at someone else's table.
fact: Chopsticks are never stuck upright in rice, because in some Asian cultures that looks like a funeral custom, and it is fine to ask for a fork.
exercise: You pass the school caretaker in the corridor. What do you do? | Greet him as kindly as you greet the head teacher | Walk straight past, as he is not one of the teachers | Nod only if he speaks first | Greet him only when you need something from him
exercise: Why do all workers deserve the same greeting? | Every worker deserves respect, whatever their job | Only important people like the head deserve greetings | Caretakers do not notice greetings | It is a rule only for teachers
exercise: How should you shake hands? | Firmly but not hard, for two or three shakes | As hard as you can squeeze, so that you look strong | With a soft, floppy hand | For as long as the other person talks
exercise: In Rotorua a Maori elder welcomes your class with a hongi. What is it? | A traditional greeting of pressing noses and foreheads | A kind of dance for tourists | A way of saying goodbye only | A greeting you should laugh at
exercise: A man in Toronto says "How's it going?" as he walks past. What do you say? | "Good, thanks!" | A long story about your week | Nothing, as it is a real question | "Why do you ask?"
exercise: How do you introduce your stepfather to your friend? | "This is my stepdad, Mark" | "That's just some man who lives here" | Say nothing and let them guess | "He lives with us, I think"
exercise: Your Australian friend says "Cheers!" as she leaves. What does she mean? | Goodbye, or thanks | She wants a cold drink | She is angry with you | She is starting a toast
exercise: Your grandma's friend is leaving after a visit. What do you do? | Walk her to the door and say goodbye | Wave from the sofa without looking up | Keep playing your game | Leave the room before she goes
exercise: You order a hot chocolate in a café. What do you say? | "Please" when you ask and "Thank you" when you get it | Just the name of the drink | "Thank you" only if it tastes good | Nothing, as it is their job
exercise: You need to ask a shop assistant where the books are. How do you start? | "Excuse me, ..." | "Oi, you over there!" | Tap them hard on the back | Shout the question from far away
exercise: The food is on the table but your uncle is still coming. What do you do? | Wait until everyone is seated and served | Start, since the food is getting cold on the plates | Eat the best bits first | Start if you are hungry enough
exercise: There is one last slice of pizza. What do you do? | Ask if anyone else would like it | Grab it quickly before anyone else | Take it, as you ate the fewest slices | Cut a tiny bit off and leave the rest
exercise: How should you eat soup at the table? | Quietly, sipping from the side of the spoon | Slurping loudly, to show the cook how much you like it | With the bowl lifted to your mouth | As fast as you can
exercise: At a friend's house you are hungry and see biscuits on the counter. What do you do? | Wait until they are offered, or ask politely | Take a few quietly while nobody in the kitchen is looking | Open the packet and share them round | Take one, since friends share
exercise: Why should chopsticks never be stuck upright in a bowl of rice? | In some Asian cultures it looks like a funeral custom | It makes the rice go cold | The chopsticks will break | It means you want more rice
test: Which shows real good manners at school? | Saying good morning to the cleaner and the head alike | Greeting only the teachers | Ignoring the dinner ladies | Speaking politely only to visitors
test: A girl gives a handshake so limp it feels like a fish. What impression may it give? | That she is unfriendly or not interested | That she is very strong and does not need to try hard | That she is very polite | That she is in a hurry
test: Which is true about the hongi? | It is a Maori greeting of shared breath, done when invited | Anyone may do it to any stranger they meet out in the street | It is a rude joke | It is a greeting used in Canada
test: A tourist in Chicago answers "How's it going?" with a ten-minute story. What did he misunderstand? | It was a quick greeting, not a real question | It needed an even longer answer with all the details | It was a question about the weather | It was a rude remark
test: Your friend's mum is leaving after picking her up from your house. What is polite? | Walking them to the door and saying goodbye | Shouting "Bye" from upstairs so they hear you go | Closing the door before they reach their car | Saying nothing at all, since they came for her
test: Which is the best way to get a busy librarian's attention? | "Excuse me, could you help me?" | Waving a book right in front of her face | Clicking your fingers | Calling "Hey, lady!"
test: At a family dinner in Galway, Gran says "Go on, start before it gets cold". What is right? | You may start, as the host said so | You still must wait for everyone | You must ask everyone's permission | You must wait for dessert
test: A boy has never used chopsticks and keeps dropping food. What is fine to do? | Ask politely for a fork | Stand the chopsticks in his rice and stop | Eat with his fingers instead | Leave the food on his plate
test: Why do we wait before taking the last biscuit on the plate? | Someone else may want it, and sharing is kind | The last one is always kept for the host to eat later | Last biscuits are bad luck | It is the smallest one
test: just a quick "good thanks" when someone walks past n says hows it going? | Yes, that's enough | No, tell them your whole day | No, stop and shake hands | No, ignore them and walk on
test: auntie in NZ says hongi, what's that | A traditional Maori greeting, done when invited | A kind of fish | A dance at weddings | A word for goodbye
test: last chip on the plate, nobody's lookin | Ask if anyone else would like it | Grab it quickly before anyone notices it's there | Lick it so it's yours | Hide it in a napkin
test: at a friends for tea, food's on the table, dive in? | Wait until food is offered or everyone is served | Dive in before it's cold | Take the biggest piece | Eat from the dish directly
explain: A boy in Chicago kept talking and laughing with his mouth full at dinner; he choked on a piece of meat and his dad had to do back blows to clear it.
explain: A girl in Sydney ignored her friend's grandmother when she said hello; the grandmother felt hurt, and the friend's family did not invite her again for a long time.
fact: Choking at the table happens most often when people talk, laugh or run while eating.
fact: If someone is choking and cannot talk or breathe, shout for help and call the emergency number; trained adults use back blows and a hard hug from behind to push the food out.
fact: Rudeness often costs friendships and invitations, even when you did not mean to be rude.
test: wanna tell my joke while eating, mouth full | Swallow first, talking with food in your mouth can make you choke | Say it fast before you swallow | Say it, but chew with your mouth shut | Laugh first and swallow after
test: Why might a family stop inviting a child who never says hello or thank you? | They feel he does not care about them | They ran out of food and could not feed another guest | Hello is old-fashioned | Children never need to greet adults
test: dad's choking at dinner and can't talk | Shout for help and call the emergency number | Give him water | Wait, it'll pass | Make him lie down
test: did u hear a kid at the wedding was laughing with his mouth full and choked on a sausage and they had to do the hug thing on him?? real lesson? | Don't talk, laugh or run while eating, to avoid choking | Only eat sausages cut into tiny bits | Never laugh at weddings at all | Drink fizzy drinks to stop choking
test: apparently that boy grabbed the last slice of pizza at the party without asking and now nobody invites him anywhere ever. what's probably exaggerated? | Nobody ever; but rudeness can cost invitations | That he took the last slice without asking anybody | That asking first is polite | That rudeness bothers people
test: my mate says that new kid's mum is so rude, she never says thank you to the dinner ladies. pass it on? | No, that's gossip about someone's family | Yes, everyone at school should know she's rude | Yes, if you only tell your friends | Yes, so the kid tells his mum off
exam: Identify the appropriate conduct at a meal before eating begins. | One waits until all are seated and served, unless the host invites one to start | One begins eating as soon as one's plate arrives | One eats first from the shared dishes | One waits until the host has finished eating
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the treatment of school staff such as caretakers and cleaners? | They are to be greeted as courteously as the head teacher | They need not be greeted at all, being occupied with their duties | They are to be greeted only by teachers | They are to be greeted only on their birthdays
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice concerning the last piece of food upon a shared plate. | One asks whether any other person would like it first | One takes it promptly, before any other person is able to do so | One divides it without asking | One leaves it untouched in all cases
exam: Ascertain the circumstances in which choking at the table most frequently occurs. | When persons talk, laugh or run while eating | When persons eat slowly and sit perfectly still at the table | When persons drink water with meals | When persons use a knife and fork
